   "l": "Speech worn to nothing — so of itself.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Speech thinned away past hearing — that is speech as it is of itself.",
   "n": "Four characters, no verb between them: a topic and what is said of it, with English forced to supply 'is'. 希 is chapter 14's own word — 聽之不聞名曰希, 'listened for and not heard: call it 希' — and Wang Bi points back to that line here, so 希言 is not merely speaking little but speech that has thinned out past hearing — and for him it is that speech which is 自然, the perfect speech of what is so of itself, not the world going its own way once you stop talking. Chapter 17 said the same of the best ruler: 悠兮其貴言, 'remote, and holding his words precious'.",
   "p": true
  },
