   "l": "So a whirling wind does not see out a morning,\na cloudburst does not see out a day.\nWho is it that makes these?\nHeaven and earth.\nEven heaven and earth cannot make it last long,\nand how much less a man?",
   "v": true,
   "i": "A wind that whirls up blows itself out before the morning is over, and a driving rain is spent before the day is. Whose doing are they? Heaven and earth's. If even heaven and earth cannot hold a thing at that pitch for long, what chance has a man?",
   "n": "而況 … 乎 is a fixed frame for arguing from the greater case to the smaller: if that much is true of heaven and earth, what of us. Note 尚: in chapter 3 (不尚賢) it was the verb 'to esteem'; here it is the adverb 'even, still', and only position tells you. 飄風 and 驟雨 stay two words each — a whirling wind, a galloping rain — because each means exactly what its parts mean. And 終, a noun meaning 'the end', is doing duty as a verb: to end a morning, to see the day out."
  },
