   "l": "the man of loss is one with loss.",
   "v": true,
   "n": "The third term does not match the other two, and the lopsidedness is real, not a translation artefact: the Way and potency are things to be at one with, loss is not. Wang Bi takes 失 of the man who has let the first two go — he is at one with his losing, and gets what that gives. The character itself is not in doubt — the Mawangdui manuscripts read 失 here as well — but what to do with it is, and readers have pulled it about ever since. We keep what our edition prints, follow Wang Bi, and say plainly that the third member is lopsided."
