   "l": "He who is one with the Way — the Way too delights to have him;\nhe who is one with potency — potency too delights to have him;\nhe who is one with loss — loss too delights to have him.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "The Way is glad of the man who is one with it; potency is glad of the man who is one with it; and loss, too, is glad of the man who is one with loss.",
   "n": "樂 is read lè, 'to take joy in'; the same character read yuè is the word for music, and the two senses have been one graph since the beginning. 之 is the object pronoun again, as it has been since chapter 1's 同謂之玄 — 'gets him', not 'of him'. The reciprocity is the point: what you keep company with keeps company with you, loss included. The Mawangdui manuscripts give this passage in a shorter and differently worded form, and the received triad may be an expansion of something plainer; the details are argued over and we do not pretend to settle them."
